\value{
An object of class \code{L0glm_fit}, with the same main components as [L0glm.fit()]:
  `coefficients`, `weights`, `dispersion`, `init.lambda`, `lambdas`,
  `family`, `z`, `X`, and `y`. Original prior weights and offsets are
  retained separately from the working weights for scoring. Methods
  \code{coef()}, \code{logLik()}, \code{AIC()}, \code{BIC()}, \code{GIC()},
  \code{EBIC()} and \code{MBIC()} are available; see \link{model_criteria}.
  \code{print()} and \code{summary()} provide compact model displays;
  see \link{model_summary}.
  \code{predict()} and \code{fitted()} return original-scale Poisson means;
  see \link{model_predictions} for new data and prediction offsets.
}
\description{
`L0nnpois()` is a fast RcppEigen/OpenMP implementation of
[L0glm.fit()] for the nonnegative Poisson model with identity link. It shares
the main fitting arguments with `L0glm.fit()` for use in deconvolution
workflows, and internally uses the sparse or dense Eigen Cholesky LLT ridge solver.
`L0glm.fit(openmp = TRUE)` selects this backend automatically for eligible
fits. Compared with the general R fitting path (`openmp = FALSE`), it keeps
the design matrix `X` in shared memory across outcome channels.
Dense inputs retain dense storage and sparse inputs retain sparse storage.
The dense solver forms weighted crossproducts using R's BLAS library.
Eligible wide active systems use the Woodbury identity to factor an
observation-space system rather than a predictor-space system.
The default penalty is `lambda = "gic"`.
Smooth IRLS updates use the same fixed-penalty backtracking safeguard as
`L0glm.fit()`, followed by the selection cutoff. Trial steps reuse predictor
differences and require no additional ridge solve.
Every coefficient, including any intercept, is constrained to be nonnegative.
The LLT update enforces this by clamping negative proposals to zero.
With positive active effects and well-chosen regularization such as
`lambda="gic"`, ridge shrinkage often keeps genuine active effects positive.
See the solver discussion in [L0glm.fit()] for measured comparisons with
the formally constrained alternatives.
This constraint cannot be disabled. Use `L0glm.fit(nonnegative = FALSE)`
if coefficients of either sign are required.
}
\examples{
## Fast nonnegative identity-link Poisson L0 or grouped L0 penalized fit
## All coefficients, including an optional intercept, are always nonnegative.
## No nonnegative, lower or upper argument is needed or accepted.
n <- p <- 1000
npeaks <- 100

sim.pois.mc <- simul(method = 4, n = n, p = p, s = npeaks / p, seed = 2)
dim(sim.pois.mc$beta_true) # 1000 x 176 channels

## Multichannel fit. OpenMP is used across outcome channels when available.
system.time(fit.mc <- L0nnpois(
  sim.pois.mc$X,
  sim.pois.mc$y,
  lambda = "gic",
  grouped = TRUE,
  relaxed = TRUE,
  parallelize = TRUE,
  ncores = round(parallel::detectCores(logical = FALSE)/2)
)) 
# with n <- p <- 1000 and npeaks <- 100   0.56s with 4 cores on my machine
# with n <- p <- 10000 and npeaks <- 1000 9.41s with 4 cores on my machine

dim(fit.mc$coefficients)
sum(fit.mc$coefficients != 0)

\dontrun{
## Compare with the general R fitting path (process-based parallelism):
system.time(fit.mc <- L0glm.fit(
  X = sim.pois.mc$X,
  y = sim.pois.mc$y,
  family = poisson(identity),
  lambda = "gic",
  nonnegative = TRUE,
  openmp = FALSE,
  grouped = TRUE,
  relaxed = TRUE,
  parallelize = TRUE,
  ncores = round(parallel::detectCores(logical = FALSE)/2)
)) 
# with n <- p <- 1000 and npeaks <- 100   11.84s with 4 cores on my machine
# with n <- p <- 10000 and npeaks <- 1000 38.55s with 4 cores on my machine

dim(fit.mc$coefficients)
sum(fit.mc$coefficients != 0)
}

## Single-channel fit uses the same nonnegative model.
system.time(fit.sc <- L0nnpois(
  sim.pois.mc$X,
  sim.pois.mc$y[, 1L],
  lambda = "gic",
  grouped = FALSE,
  relaxed = FALSE,
  parallelize = FALSE
)) 
# with n <- p <- 1000 and npeaks <- 100   0.03s on my machine
# with n <- p <- 10000 and npeaks <- 1000 0.13s on my machine

sum(fit.sc$coefficients != 0)

## Native validation stopping with an independent repeat measurement.
small <- simul(n = 300L, p = 300L, n_nonzero = 20L, m = 4L, seed = 7L)
set.seed(8)
y.validation <- matrix(rpois(length(small$y_true), small$y_true), 300L, 4L)
validated <- L0nnpois(small$X, small$y, lambda = "gic", ncores = 2L,
  validation = list(X = small$X, y = y.validation, patience = 3L))
validated$validation$deviance
validated$validation$selection$history

## Compare with the general R path for a single channel:
system.time(fit.sc <- L0glm.fit(
  X = sim.pois.mc$X,
  y = sim.pois.mc$y[, 1L],
  family = poisson(identity),
  lambda = "gic",
  nonnegative = TRUE,
  openmp = FALSE,
  grouped = FALSE,
  relaxed = FALSE,
  parallelize = FALSE
)) 
# with n <- p <- 1000 and npeaks <- 100   0.04s on my machine
# with n <- p <- 10000 and npeaks <- 1000 0.25s on my machine

sum(fit.sc$coefficients != 0)
}
